// Easy to misuse: booleans with hidden meaning, implicit units, silent failure
void process(bool buy, double qty, double px);

// Hard to misuse: explicit domain types, validated, one obvious way to call
enum class Side { Buy, Sell };

struct Fill {
    Side side;
    int  quantity;    // always positive, in shares
    double price;     // per share, in the instrument's currency
};

// Returns the updated position or throws std::invalid_argument on a bad fill
Position apply_fill(PositionBook& book, const Fill& fill);

#include <memory>

class FeeModel {                                  // Strategy interface
public:
    virtual ~FeeModel() = default;
    virtual double fee(double notional, int shares) const = 0;
};

class PerShareFee : public FeeModel {
public:
    explicit PerShareFee(double cents_per_share) : cps_(cents_per_share / 100.0) {}
    double fee(double, int shares) const override { return shares * cps_; }
private:
    double cps_;                                  // dollars per share
};

class BpsFee : public FeeModel {
public:
    explicit BpsFee(double bps) : bps_(bps) {}
    double fee(double notional, int) const override { return notional * bps_ / 10000.0; }
private:
    double bps_;
};

class OrderCostCalculator {                       // context depends on the abstraction
public:
    explicit OrderCostCalculator(std::unique_ptr<FeeModel> model)
        : model_(std::move(model)) {}

    double total(double price, int shares) const {
        const double notional = price * shares;
        return notional + model_->fee(notional, shares);
    }
private:
    std::unique_ptr<FeeModel> model_;
};

guidedExercise.prompt:
Refactor this branching fee logic into the Strategy pattern. Before:

```cpp
double fee(const std::string& model, double notional, int shares) {
    if (model == "PER_SHARE") return 0.02 * shares;
    if (model == "BPS")       return notional * 1.0 / 10000.0;
    throw std::invalid_argument("unknown fee model");
}
```

Give the interface, two concrete strategies, and state which SOLID principles the refactor serves and what the caller must change.

guidedExercise.solution:
```cpp
class FeeModel {
public:
    virtual ~FeeModel() = default;
    virtual double fee(double notional, int shares) const = 0;
};

class PerShareFee : public FeeModel {
public:
    explicit PerShareFee(double cents_per_share) : cps_(cents_per_share / 100.0) {}
    double fee(double, int shares) const override { return shares * cps_; }
private:
    double cps_;
};

class BpsFee : public FeeModel {
public:
    explicit BpsFee(double bps) : bps_(bps) {}
    double fee(double notional, int) const override { return notional * bps_ / 10000.0; }
private:
    double bps_;
};

// caller: OrderCostCalculator calc(std::make_unique<BpsFee>(1.0));
//         calc.total(42.17, 300);   // -> 12651.00 + 1.2651 = 12652.2651
```

The string parameter (a misuse magnet: 'bps' vs 'BPS') disappears into the type system; an unknown model is now a compile-time impossibility rather than a runtime throw. Served principles: **Open/Closed** (new model = new class, callers untouched), **Dependency Inversion** (callers depend on FeeModel, not on a dispatch function), and **Single Responsibility** (each model owns only its math). The caller changes once - it now constructs and holds a strategy - and never changes again.

independentExercise.solution:
```cpp
enum class Side { Buy, Sell };

struct Fill {
    Side   side;
    int    quantity;   // contract: > 0
    double price;      // per share
};

struct Position {
    long long quantity = 0;   // signed: positive = long, negative = short
    double    avg_cost = 0.0;
};

class PositionBook {
public:
    virtual ~PositionBook() = default;
    // Returns the post-fill position; throws std::invalid_argument on invalid fill.
    virtual Position apply(const Fill& fill) = 0;
};
```

Justifications: `enum class Side` makes 'B' or 'buy' unrepresentable (**hard to misuse**); `int quantity` with a documented positive contract means sign lives in *one* place - the book (**Single Responsibility** of sign handling); the signed `Position::quantity` models shorts without a separate field; the explicit `std::invalid_argument` beats a silent bool (**explicit error path**); the abstract base with a virtual `apply` is the **Dependency Inversion** seam - a test injects a fake `PositionBook` into, say, a `RiskMonitor` under test, with no database involved. Sign convention of the arithmetic (BUY adds, SELL subtracts) is unit-tested exactly as in the testing lesson: buy 100 then sell 100 returns to 0.

